  [NEW · SERIOUS · FACT] The draft asserts MONALEESA-2's final OS p-value is one-sided, but the NEJM publication and multiple secondary sources print it as two-sided P = 0.008.
     quote         : “The test is one-sided, at a cumulative 2.5% level of significance, per the trial's own results posting on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT01958021)”
     why           : The NEJM paper (the primary publication this page cites for the OS result) prints 'two-sided P = 0.008', as do the ESMO abstract and OncLive reporting of the same analysis. The draft resolves the conflict by preferring the registry over the journal, but it does not acknowledge that the journal — the source it cites for the HR and medians — says the opposite. A reader who checks the NEJM paper will find a direct contradiction with what this page states. The draft's own correction history records that an earlier version called it two-sided 'without a source'; the current version calls it one-sided and cites the registry, but the journal is an equally valid source and it disagrees. The conflict is real and unresolved, and the page presents one side as settled.
     fix           : Acknowledge the conflict explicitly: the NEJM publication prints 'two-sided P = 0.008'; the ClinicalTrials.gov posting describes a one-sided test. State that the two sources disagree and that the page cannot resolve it from the documents it can open, rather than declaring the registry the winner.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] The draft states the MONALEESA-2 OS p-value is 'p = 0.008' in the main table and body, but the ClinicalTrials.gov posting it relies on for the one-sided claim gives HR 0.765 and p = 0.004, not 0.008.
     quote         : “63.9 vs 51.4 mo HR 0.76 (0.63–0.93) final analysis, 6.6 y, NEJM 2022 · p = 0.008 — significant”
     why           : The draft uses the registry to establish that the test is one-sided, and quotes the registry in the sources section: 'HR 0.765 (95% CI 0.628–0.932), p = 0.004'. But the main table and the body of the piece print p = 0.008, which is the NEJM paper's figure. The draft is simultaneously citing the registry for the test direction and the journal for the p-value — two sources that give different p-values for the same analysis (0.004 vs 0.008). The page does not explain this discrepancy or tell the reader which figure it is using and why. A reader checking the registry will find 0.004; a reader checking the journal will find 0.008; neither will find an explanation.
     fix           : Either use the registry's p-value (0.004) consistently if the registry is the authoritative source, or use the journal's (0.008) and note that the registry gives a different figure. Do not use the registry to establish test direction while using the journal's p-value without explaining why the two sources are being mixed.

  [NEW · MINOR · FACT] The draft describes the Shaaban trial as randomised 'in 29 blocks of four by opaque sealed envelope', but the journal paper describes randomisation in blocks of four — the number of blocks (29) is not stated in the abstract or ESMO poster and may be an error introduced from a misread of the full text.
     quote         : “the randomisation in 29 blocks of four by opaque sealed envelope”
     why           : The ESMO poster and the journal abstract confirm the trial design and the 116 patients (58 per arm), but neither publicly available source states '29 blocks'. If this figure comes from the full text (which the draft says it read), it should be verifiable — but if it is a misread (e.g. a table row number or a different figure), it is a specific numerical claim that cannot be checked by a reader without full-text access. The draft should either confirm the source precisely or omit the block count.
     fix           : Confirm the '29 blocks' figure against the full text and add a note of where in the paper it appears, or remove the block count and retain only 'blocks of four by opaque sealed envelope', which is confirmable from public sources.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says the Tanguy 2018 paper argued 'that MONARCH 3 was less powered than PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA-2 to detect a survival difference', but the paper's own conclusion is that PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA trials have 'almost similar power' while MONARCH-3 has 'more limited power' — which is consistent — but the draft omits that Tanguy also found PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA-2 similarly powered, which matters because the draft elsewhere treats PALOMA-2 as uniquely powered for survival.
     quote         : “Tanguy and colleagues made it formally in npj Breast Cancer in 2018, before any of these trials had mature survival data, arguing that MONARCH 3 was less powered than PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA-2 to detect a survival difference”
     why           : The Tanguy paper's finding is that PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA-2 have 'almost similar power' and MONARCH-3 has more limited power. The draft cites Tanguy selectively to support the MONARCH 3 power point, but Tanguy's equal treatment of PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA-2 cuts against the draft's own argument that PALOMA-2 was uniquely 'powered for survival' while MONALEESA-2 was not. The draft uses Tanguy to support one half of its argument while the same paper complicates the other half.
     fix           : Note that Tanguy found PALOMA-2 and MONALEESA-2 similarly powered, and MONARCH-3 less so — and acknowledge that this sits in some tension with the draft's own distinction between PALOMA-2 (powered) and MONALEESA-2 (not discussed as powered) in the trial-power paragraph.

  [NEW · MINOR · FACT] The draft says the PALMARES-2 registry record is 'still recruiting toward an estimated 3,500 patients', but the ClinicalTrials.gov record shows an estimated primary completion date of 2030 and does not state a target enrolment of 3,500.
     quote         : “its ClinicalTrials.gov record (NCT06805812, still recruiting toward an estimated 3,500 patients) has no results posted”
     why           : The ClinicalTrials.gov record for NCT06805812 confirms the study is recruiting and has no results posted, but the enrolment target of 3,500 is not visible in the publicly available record from the search results, which show the study cohort at 1,982 patients in published sub-analyses. If 3,500 is the registered target it should be verifiable; if it is an estimate from a conference presentation or the paper itself (which the draft cannot open), the source should be named.
     fix           : Name the source for the 3,500 figure. If it comes from the registry, confirm it. If it comes from a document the draft cannot open, say so.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft states 'the guideline also records that the three drugs have not been directly compared in clinical trials' and later scopes this to first-line aromatase-inhibitor combinations — but the draft does not check whether the guideline's sentence is itself scoped that way or is a broader statement that the draft is re-scoping on the guideline's behalf.
     quote         : “The guideline also records that the three drugs have not been directly compared in clinical trials.”
     why           : The draft later says 'The guideline's own sentence, that the CDK4/6 inhibitors have not been directly compared in clinical trials, is about first-line aromatase-inhibitor combinations and is accurate in that scope.' But the draft cannot open the guideline through an AI tool (it says so in the sources), and the scoping of the guideline's sentence to first-line AI combinations is the draft's own interpretation. If the guideline's sentence is broader — a general statement about the class — then the draft's defence of it ('accurate in that scope') is the draft doing the scoping, not the guideline. This matters because the draft uses the guideline's accuracy as a rhetorical anchor.
     fix           : Quote the guideline's exact sentence and let the reader see whether the scope is the guideline's or the draft's. If the sentence is unscoped in the guideline, say so rather than attributing the scope to the guideline.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says 'an earlier version of this page said none of these trials was powered for overall survival, which is false' — but then says 'Survival was a secondary endpoint in all three', which is true but risks re-introducing the same flattening it just corrected, since PALOMA-2's survival analysis was both a secondary endpoint and a powered one.
     quote         : “Survival was a secondary endpoint in all three — which, as the paragraph above shows, covers both a powered analysis and an unpowered one.”
     why           : The draft corrects the error and immediately adds the clarification in the same sentence, so this is not a repetition of the error. But the sentence structure — leading with 'secondary endpoint in all three' before the clarification — risks a reader who skims taking away the flattened version. The clarification is present but subordinated.
     fix           : Lead with the distinction rather than the similarity: 'Among the three, PALOMA-2's survival analysis was both a secondary endpoint and a powered one; MONARCH 3's was a secondary endpoint but not powered for it. Calling all three secondary endpoints covers both cases and obscures the difference this paragraph is about.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says the Shaaban trial 'registered as NCT05670054' — this number is confirmed by the ESMO poster — but the draft also says it 'ran July 2022 to December 2023' and was 'single-centre', details it attributes to the full text. The ESMO poster confirms Mansoura University, Egypt as the single centre and the dates, so this is not an error, but the draft should note that the single-centre design is a material limit on generalisability that it does not discuss.
     quote         : “the trial is single-centre, it ran July 2022 to December 2023, its power calculation is on response rate, and progression-free survival is a secondary endpoint”
     why           : The draft lists 'single-centre' as a factual disclosure in the sources section but does not discuss what it means for the result in the body of the piece. A single-centre trial of 116 patients is a materially weaker basis for a 'no difference' conclusion than a multi-centre trial, and the draft uses this trial as one of two head-to-head randomised comparisons. The body says 'neither separated them' without flagging that one of the two is a 116-patient single-centre study.
     fix           : In the body section where the Shaaban trial is discussed, note that it is single-centre and powered on response rate, not progression-free survival, so its 'no difference' finding on PFS carries less weight than the design label 'randomised phase III' implies.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] The draft says 'the two randomised trials that have put any pair of them head to head, both ribociclib against palbociclib, found no difference' in the 'What a reader is owed' section — but HARMONIA terminated at 61 patients and has no results publication, so it cannot be said to have 'found no difference'.
     quote         : “the two randomised trials that have put any pair of them head to head, both ribociclib against palbociclib, found no difference”
     why           : The draft correctly says in the body and the 'Established' section that HARMONIA 'terminated with 61 patients' and that 'this page draws none from it beyond the fact that the trial was run.' But in the 'What a reader is owed' section — the section a patient reads — it says both trials 'found no difference', which attributes a finding to HARMONIA that the draft itself says it has no results for. This is an internal contradiction and, in the section most likely to be read by a patient making a treatment decision, it overstates the evidence.
     fix           : Change to: 'the one randomised trial that produced results, Shaaban and colleagues in the second line with fulvestrant, found no difference; HARMONIA terminated at 61 patients and reported no results.'

  [NEW · MINOR · FACT] The draft characterises P-VERIFY as funded by Pfizer and discloses this, but does not disclose that PALMARES-2's investigators include authors with declared conflicts of interest involving Pfizer, Novartis, and Lilly — which the draft says it cannot see because the paper is behind a paywall, but which are visible in the sub-analysis publications it could reach.
     quote         : “We could not open PALMARES-2's declaration of interests. That is a statement about our access and not about its investigators, of whom we know nothing and about whom this page will not speculate.”
     why           : The draft says it knows nothing about PALMARES-2's investigators' interests. But the sub-analysis papers for PALMARES-2 (NCT06805812), which the draft's own search could have reached, list conflicts of interest for named investigators including personal fees from Pfizer, Novartis, and Lilly. The draft's claim that it 'knows nothing' about the investigators' interests is not accurate — it knows what the sub-analyses disclose. This does not make PALMARES-2's findings wrong, but the draft's symmetry argument ('we read one study's funding and not the other's, so neither is offered as the disinterested one') rests on a false premise.
     fix           : Remove the claim that 'we know nothing' about PALMARES-2's investigators. Note that sub-analyses of the same study list conflicts including fees from multiple manufacturers, and that the same disclosure caveat therefore applies to PALMARES-2 as to P-VERIFY.

